              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Arabic for literacy, then the Arabic for attendance."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: إلا إذا",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "إلا إذا — illā idhā — \"unless\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "لن نخرج إلا إذا توقف المطر. lan nakhruja illā idhā tawaqqafa al-maṭar. We will not go out unless the rain stops."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "After إذا the verb is usually in the past, even for the future."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "إلا إذا (illā idhā) joins the native Arabic particles illā, \"except\", and idhā, \"if\": except if, unless. The deeper origin of such particles is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for saying what happens if."
            }
          ]
        },
